      "math_academy_id": "q-184002",
      "sequence_position": 20,
      "problem": "Suppose $n=8-2p-6{q}^{2}-4pq,$ where $p,q\\in Z.$ Complete the following argument.\n\nThe number $n$ is {{field-1}} since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =8-2p-6{q}^{2}-4pq \\\\  & =2(4-{{field-2}}-2pq) \\\\  & ={{field-3}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere  $a=${{field-4}}.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *even* if it can be written as $2a,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNotice that all terms in the expression $8-2p-6{q}^{2}-4pq$ have a common factor of $2.$ \n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\text{even}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =8-2p-6{q}^{2}-4pq \\\\  & =2({{4-p-3{q}^{2}-2pq}_{⏟}}_{a}) \\\\  & =2a,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=4-p-3{q}^{2}-2pq\\in Z.$",
